WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:02.334
-Steve Carell.
-Wow.

00:00:02.334 --> 00:00:03.734
-It's so good to see you, buddy.
-I am really something.

00:00:03.734 --> 00:00:05.701
-You really are something.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:05.701 --> 00:00:07.801
Thank you for doing
"Go On, Git."

00:00:07.801 --> 00:00:09.467
I appreciate that.
-Oh, my gosh, no problem.

00:00:09.467 --> 00:00:11.701
-I love doing any sketch work
with you.

00:00:11.701 --> 00:00:13.501
Any time we do a bit together,
it always makes laugh.

00:00:13.501 --> 00:00:15.000
-That's --
Those are our roots.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:17.300
-Yeah, that's right.
You started in Second City...

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:19.701
-Yeah.
-...doing sketch comedy.

00:00:19.701 --> 00:00:23.000
Any characters you remember
doing over there?

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:25.434
-A lot of forgettable things.
The one thing that --

00:00:25.434 --> 00:00:26.667
[ Laughter ]

00:00:26.667 --> 00:00:28.767
No, I mean, a lot of
it's just very disposable.

00:00:28.767 --> 00:00:30.000
-Yeah.
-You know, you do it one night,

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:31.167
and then you never see it again,

00:00:31.167 --> 00:00:33.033
or you think about it,
but it never comes back.

00:00:33.033 --> 00:00:35.334
There was one character
I was working on,

00:00:35.334 --> 00:00:37.234
and it was a guy who --

00:00:37.234 --> 00:00:39.968
It was a poker scene, and I kept
trying to get it into the show.

00:00:39.968 --> 00:00:42.334
It never went into a show.
It was a poker scene,

00:00:42.334 --> 00:00:46.000
and the guy, and all of his
friends, are talking about sex.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:48.167
And they're trying to regale
each other with these --

00:00:48.167 --> 00:00:51.033
you know, these stories
of sexual conquest.

00:00:51.033 --> 00:00:55.000
And my guy didn't
have any sort of context.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:58.100
You know, no frame of reference,
and obviously was trying to lie.

00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:00.701
So that became
"40-Year-Old Virgin."

00:01:00.701 --> 00:01:01.968
-Really?
-That whole idea --

00:01:01.968 --> 00:01:05.567
Yeah, Judd Apatow,
after we did "Anchorman,"

00:01:05.567 --> 00:01:07.534
he came to me and said,
"Hey, would you like to,

00:01:07.534 --> 00:01:09.868
you know, get together?
Do you have any ideas to pitch?"

00:01:09.868 --> 00:01:12.767
And we got together,
I pitched all these other ideas,

00:01:12.767 --> 00:01:14.868
and just before I left, I said,
"You know what?

00:01:14.868 --> 00:01:16.968
There's one more.
There was this thing I was

00:01:16.968 --> 00:01:18.634
kind of trying to get going
at Second City

00:01:18.634 --> 00:01:20.267
that never went anywhere."
-No way.

00:01:20.267 --> 00:01:21.601
-He heard that, and he said,

00:01:21.601 --> 00:01:24.667
"That I could sell, like,
immediately."

00:01:24.667 --> 00:01:28.400
And the next week, he talked
to an executive, sold it.

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:29.968
We wrote it.
It got greenlit.

00:01:29.968 --> 00:01:32.300
It was like -- it was -- yeah.
-The rest is history.

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:34.267
From a character that never made
it to stage at Second City.

00:01:34.267 --> 00:01:37.400
-Right. Yeah.
-I wanted to show you something.

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:39.000
I found something funny
on the Internet.

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:40.734
This is going around.
People say --

00:01:40.734 --> 00:01:42.701
They send me things all the time
and say people look like me

00:01:42.701 --> 00:01:43.901
and all that stuff.

00:01:43.901 --> 00:01:45.901
Someone says you have
a doppelganger out there.

00:01:45.901 --> 00:01:48.934
And I don't know if you've ever
seen this, but it's pretty good.

00:01:48.934 --> 00:01:51.467
People say that
you look like Alice Cooper.

00:01:51.467 --> 00:01:54.167
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:01:54.167 --> 00:01:56.367
Not bad.
-Yeah.

00:01:56.367 --> 00:01:57.901
♪♪

00:01:57.901 --> 00:01:59.501
Yeah.
-It's kind of fantastic.

00:01:59.501 --> 00:02:00.767
-I see it.
-In that photo --

00:02:00.767 --> 00:02:02.634
that's a perfect photo,
right there, yeah.

00:02:02.634 --> 00:02:04.601
[ Laughter ]

00:02:04.601 --> 00:02:06.100
Have you ever met Alice Cooper?

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:07.734
-I met Alice Cooper.

00:02:07.734 --> 00:02:10.067
I waited on him
when I was working in Chicago.

00:02:10.067 --> 00:02:11.467
-Did you really?
-When I was waiting tables.

00:02:11.467 --> 00:02:12.868
Yeah, I was working
at a restaurant.

00:02:12.868 --> 00:02:15.067
He came in, and, he was --
you know, he was Alice Cooper.

00:02:15.067 --> 00:02:16.834
-Yeah.
-And couldn't have been nicer.

00:02:16.834 --> 00:02:18.067
Sweetest guy in the world.

00:02:18.067 --> 00:02:19.434
-That is the thing
about Alice Cooper.

00:02:19.434 --> 00:02:22.934
I've met him once before, too,
and he could be whatever.

00:02:22.934 --> 00:02:24.901
-Yeah.
-He could be a rock guy

00:02:24.901 --> 00:02:27.801
who's like, "Whatever, man."
He is the nicest human.

00:02:27.801 --> 00:02:29.501
-Nicest guy.
And I go, "Oh, my God.

00:02:29.501 --> 00:02:30.634
I love Alice Cooper.

00:02:30.634 --> 00:02:32.300
I just want to hang out
with the guy."

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:33.701
But, I mean,
he walks into a room,

00:02:33.701 --> 00:02:35.834
and he's unmistakably
Alice Cooper.

00:02:35.834 --> 00:02:39.767
-Yeah.
-Steven Tyler is from an area

00:02:39.767 --> 00:02:41.534
that I live in in Massachusetts,

00:02:41.534 --> 00:02:43.868
and he always dresses
like Steven Tyler.

00:02:43.868 --> 00:02:46.033
He wears, like, you know,
leopard-skin pants,

00:02:46.033 --> 00:02:47.167
and he walks around these --

00:02:47.167 --> 00:02:48.934
This little town
outside of Boston.

00:02:48.934 --> 00:02:50.400
And he goes
into the Dunkin' Donuts,

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:51.834
buys doughnuts for everybody.

00:02:51.834 --> 00:02:53.400
He's like, "Ooh-ahh, doughnuts!"

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:56.167
-Yeah, yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:56.167 --> 00:02:59.133
He's got scarves and stuff.
-But he's like the nicest guy.

00:02:59.133 --> 00:03:01.000
The same thing.
-Yeah. How do they do that?

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:03.334
-I think they own it.
I think you have to own --

00:03:03.334 --> 00:03:05.334
If you're Alice Cooper, you have
to own being Alice Cooper.

00:03:05.334 --> 00:03:07.267
-Yeah. 'Cause that's --
-You own being Steven Tyler.

00:03:07.267 --> 00:03:08.400
-That's who he is.
-Yeah.

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:10.467
-Anyone else famous
that you waited on?

00:03:10.467 --> 00:03:11.767
-I've only --

00:03:11.767 --> 00:03:13.801
You're the only other famous
person I've ever met.

00:03:13.801 --> 00:03:15.968
[ Laughter ]

00:03:15.968 --> 00:03:18.100
-I'm so happy.
-I waited on --

00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:20.467
Do you remember --
A lot of your audience

00:03:20.467 --> 00:03:23.334
won't know this reference.
Remember Clara Peller?

00:03:23.334 --> 00:03:25.133
"Where's the beef?"
-"Where's the beef?" lady.

00:03:25.133 --> 00:03:26.734
-Yeah.
-Oh, Clara Peller.

00:03:26.734 --> 00:03:29.200
Oh, my gosh.
You waited on Clara Peller?

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:31.033
-Yeah. Yeah.
-[ Gasps ]

00:03:31.033 --> 00:03:32.834
And guess what she ordered.

00:03:32.834 --> 00:03:34.767
-Please don't say a hamburger.

00:03:34.767 --> 00:03:36.734
-A hamburger!
-Wow!

00:03:36.734 --> 00:03:38.734
♪♪

00:03:38.734 --> 00:03:40.501
-Yeah.

00:03:40.501 --> 00:03:43.334
And she had the biggest
entourage I have ever seen.

00:03:43.334 --> 00:03:45.167
[ Laughter ]
She had like 20 people.

00:03:45.167 --> 00:03:46.601
-20 people, yeah.
-Oh, yeah.

00:03:46.601 --> 00:03:48.400
-And like, "Hey, by the way,
she's not gonna say it."

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:50.534
-"Don't ask her to say it."

00:03:50.534 --> 00:03:52.200
-"Don't ask her to say it.
She's not gonna say it."

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:53.567
And then you go,
"Come on, Clara."

00:03:53.567 --> 00:03:54.734
-"Come on.
Give me one.

00:03:54.734 --> 00:03:56.400
Just give me a little
"Where's the beef?"

00:03:56.400 --> 00:03:59.100
-Where's the beef?
That's so good.

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:01.734
-I want to talk about
"Minions: Rise of Gru."

00:04:01.734 --> 00:04:04.167
This one -- I told you
backstage, I really mean it --

00:04:04.167 --> 00:04:05.567
is I think this one's
my favorite one.

00:04:05.567 --> 00:04:08.133
I love all of them, but this
one's a fantastic movie.

00:04:08.133 --> 00:04:09.734
Crushed in front of my kids.

00:04:09.734 --> 00:04:11.100
-Oh, good.
-They loved it so much.

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:13.667
But I loved it, as well.
Do people come up to you

00:04:13.667 --> 00:04:16.968
and ask you to do Gru for, like,
a voicemail for their kids.

00:04:16.968 --> 00:04:18.400
-Definitely for that.

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:21.067
I don't like to do it, like,
especially around little kids,

00:04:21.067 --> 00:04:23.234
because to associate --

00:04:23.234 --> 00:04:24.534
You don't want to associate me
with that voice.

00:04:24.534 --> 00:04:25.934
-They don't get it.
-It's a little confusing.

00:04:25.934 --> 00:04:27.067
-They don't get it.
-A little, like,

00:04:27.067 --> 00:04:30.334
"What is this guy doing?
That's not -- it's him."

00:04:30.334 --> 00:04:32.868
But friends, and I've done it
for charities and stuff,

00:04:32.868 --> 00:04:35.000
to leave a voice message.

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:38.334
And, like, this friend of mine,
their son Dylan,

00:04:38.334 --> 00:04:39.367
"Oh, it's his birthday.

00:04:39.367 --> 00:04:41.033
Could you leave a message
as Gru."

00:04:41.033 --> 00:04:44.367
"Hi, Dylan, I hope you have
a terrible birthday!"

00:04:44.367 --> 00:04:45.434
[ Laughter ]

00:04:45.434 --> 00:04:47.901
"Be a good kid
and brush your teeth."

00:04:47.901 --> 00:04:49.934
[ Laughter ]
And I remember --

00:04:49.934 --> 00:04:53.100
So, the mom called me
the day after and said,

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:56.200
"You know what?
He looked at me

00:04:56.200 --> 00:04:59.200
after I played that message and
said, 'Mom, see? He's real.'"

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:00.868
-Oh, see? Come on.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:00.868 --> 00:05:02.501
-Like, that's the coolest thing.

00:05:02.501 --> 00:05:06.100
-In this one, you play Gru
at like a 12-year-old

00:05:06.100 --> 00:05:07.367
or an 11-year-old.
-Yeah.

00:05:07.367 --> 00:05:08.634
-And how did you
find that voice?

00:05:08.634 --> 00:05:10.067
The younger Gru.
-Oh, it was so,

00:05:10.067 --> 00:05:11.567
so difficult, Jimmy.

00:05:11.567 --> 00:05:14.868
[ Laughter ]

00:05:14.868 --> 00:05:16.868
Like, "How do I do this?"
-Yeah, how do you do --

00:05:16.868 --> 00:05:18.234
-Oh, I'll go from this...

00:05:18.234 --> 00:05:19.400
[ High-pitched voice ]
...to this.

00:05:19.400 --> 00:05:21.000
[ Laughter ]
-There you go. That's amazing.

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:22.934
-See?
-Do you mind leaving a word --

00:05:22.934 --> 00:05:23.934
-Thank you.

00:05:23.934 --> 00:05:26.033
-Can you leave a message
for my --

00:05:26.033 --> 00:05:28.834
My daughter's birthday's
in September -- as young Gru.

00:05:28.834 --> 00:05:30.567
-[ Laughs ]
-No.

00:05:30.567 --> 00:05:32.434
You brought, actually,
your son to the premiere,

00:05:32.434 --> 00:05:35.334
who, your son, I can't even
believe now, is 18.

00:05:35.334 --> 00:05:36.901
-18, yeah.
-I can't believe that.

00:05:36.901 --> 00:05:39.200
-Yeah, my son, 18,
my daughter, 21,

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:41.868
came to the premiere.
And they wanted to go.

00:05:41.868 --> 00:05:44.834
It wasn't like, "Please, come."
They were the ones who --

00:05:44.834 --> 00:05:46.234
No, seriously.
-It was never "Please, come"?

00:05:46.234 --> 00:05:49.367
-No, no, for me -- I wasn't,
like, begging them to come.

00:05:49.367 --> 00:05:52.167
I was like, "I get it.
If you think it's a kids movie,

00:05:52.167 --> 00:05:54.400
and you don't want to -- I get
it if you don't want to come."

00:05:54.400 --> 00:05:56.667
They've grown up with it.
They love it.

00:05:56.667 --> 00:05:59.200
They love the whole series.
They were 4 and 7

00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:02.267
when the first one came out.
So they really --

00:06:02.267 --> 00:06:04.300
-And they helped you
get the voice.

00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:05.901
-They helped pick the voice,
yeah.

00:06:05.901 --> 00:06:07.400
I tried out
some different voices,

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:08.968
and that's the one
that made them laugh.

00:06:08.968 --> 00:06:11.000
But, yeah, it was so cool
to have them there.

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:12.100
And they loved it.

00:06:12.100 --> 00:06:14.067
-Yeah.
I want to show everyone a clip.

00:06:14.067 --> 00:06:15.767
It is a great movie.
Here's Steve Carell

00:06:15.767 --> 00:06:18.767
in "Minions: The Rise of Gru."
Take a look at this.

00:06:18.767 --> 00:06:20.234
♪♪

00:06:20.234 --> 00:06:22.334
-Oh! Not now!
Come on!

00:06:22.334 --> 00:06:24.200
-Aah!

00:06:24.200 --> 00:06:26.367
-[ Shouts ]

00:06:26.367 --> 00:06:27.367
[ Metal crunching ]

00:06:27.367 --> 00:06:28.901
-Oh!

00:06:28.901 --> 00:06:30.133
-Hyah!

00:06:30.133 --> 00:06:31.734
-[ Grunts ]

00:06:31.734 --> 00:06:37.300
[ All shouting ]

00:06:39.067 --> 00:06:40.167
-Aah!

00:06:40.167 --> 00:06:41.534
[ Crashing ]

00:06:41.534 --> 00:06:42.534
[ Tires screech ]

00:06:42.534 --> 00:06:45.968
-Yeah, baby.

00:06:45.968 --> 00:06:47.234
Aha!

00:06:47.234 --> 00:06:49.634
I'll handle this.

00:06:49.634 --> 00:06:50.467
-Wha!
-Aah!

00:06:50.467 --> 00:06:51.601
-[ Laughing ]

00:06:51.601 --> 00:06:52.601
[ Grunts ]

00:06:52.601 --> 00:06:55.300
♪♪

00:06:55.300 --> 00:06:58.100
-Hope you enjoy
the rest of your short life.

00:06:58.100 --> 00:06:59.667
-Please work.

00:06:59.667 --> 00:07:02.400
Come on, come on, come on.

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:04.334
-[ Gasps ]

00:07:04.334 --> 00:07:06.033
-Yeah!

00:07:06.033 --> 00:07:07.367
-That's right.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:07.367 --> 00:07:09.501
Come on.
Steve Carell, everybody!

00:07:09.501 --> 00:07:13.467
"Minions: The Rise of Gru"
is in theaters this is Friday.

